  "worked_solution": "Note that the differential equation is of the form\n\n$$\nP(x,y)\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\text{d}y=0\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y$ and $Q(x,y)=6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y.$\n\nSince the equation is exact, our solution $u(x,y)$ must satisfy the following system:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202x}=P(x,y),\\,\\frac{\u2202u}{\u2202y}=Q(x,y)\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate $P(x,y)$ with respect to $x.$ Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =\\int (3{y}^{2}-6{x}^{2}y)\\,\\text{d}x+f(y) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we integrate $Q(x,y)$ with respect to $y.$  Note that the solution should contain an arbitrary function of $x.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(x,y) & =\\int Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =\\int (6xy-2{x}^{3}-\\operatorname{sin}\u2061y)\\,\\text{d}y+g(x) \\\\  & =3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y+g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComparing the two solutions, we see that we must have\n\n$$\ng(x)=0,\\,f(y)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution $u(x,y)=c$ is\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y=c.\n$$\n\nThe particular solution specified by the initial condition must have $y=0$ when $x=1.$ These values determine the value of the constant $c.$ Substituting this into the equation gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y=c \\\\ 3\\cdot (1)\\cdot (0)^{2}-2\\cdot (1)^{3}\\cdot (0)+\\operatorname{cos}\u20610=c \\\\ 0+0+1=c \\\\ c=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n3x{y}^{2}-2{x}^{3}y+\\operatorname{cos}\u2061y=1.\n$$",
